  "prediction": "The report outlines various nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries that have been approved through different legislative processes. The Atomic Energy Act of 1954, as amended, typically requires a specific legislative process for approving nuclear cooperation agreements. However, there have been instances where nuclear cooperation agreements have been approved outside of this process.\n\nOne such instance was the U.S.-Australia nuclear cooperation agreement, which was approved through legislation even though there were sufficient legislative days remaining for the agreement to enter into force without it. Another example was the U.S.-China nuclear cooperation agreement, which required presidential certifications and reports followed by a period of 30 days of continuous session of Congress for approval.\n\nThe report also mentions the nuclear cooperation agreement between the United States and India, which was permitted under P.L. 109-401 and did not require the usual legislative process. Lastly, the report mentions an extension of the U.S.-Norway nuclear cooperation agreement, which was approved through legislation despite concerns about insufficient legislative days remaining in the 114th Congress. Overall, the report highlights the flexibility in the legislative process for approving nuclear cooperation agreements and the various ways in which these agreements have been approved.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines various nuclear cooperation agreements between the United States and other countries that have been approved through different legislative processes. The Atomic Energy Act of 1954, as amended, typically requires a specific legislative process for approving nuclear cooperation agreements. However, there have been instances where nuclear cooperation agreements have been approved outside of this process.\n\nOne such instance was the U.S.-Australia nuclear cooperation agreement, which was approved through legislation even though there were sufficient legislative days remaining for the agreement to enter into force without it. Another example was the U.S.-China nuclear cooperation agreement, which required presidential certifications and reports followed by a period of 30 days of continuous session of Congress for approval.\n\nThe report also mentions the nuclear cooperation agreement between the United States and India, which was permitted under P.L. 109-401 and did not require the usual legislative process. Lastly, the report mentions an extension of the U.S.-Norway nuclear cooperation agreement, which was approved through legislation despite concerns about insufficient legislative days remaining in the 114th Congress. Overall, the report highlights the flexibility in the legislative process for approving nuclear cooperation agreements and the various ways in which these agreements have been approved.",
  "score": 27.5862064229753,
  "answers": [
    "In order for the United States to engage in significant civilian nuclear cooperation with other states, it must conclude a framework agreement that meets specific requirements under Section 123 of the Atomic Energy Act (AEA). Significant nuclear cooperation includes the export of reactors, critical parts of reactors, and reactor fuel. The AEA also provides for export control licensing procedures and criteria for terminating cooperation. Congressional review is required for Section 123 agreements; the AEA establishes special parliamentary procedures by which Congress may act on a proposed agreement."
  ],
